# ── G-15-4 gap closure (15-13) — suppress the autostarted NetworkManager
#    secret agent. This file exists for that and nothing else. ──────────
#
# WHY THIS LIVES IN THE `quickshell` PACKAGE
# The suppression exists *because* the quickshell wifi panel took over this
# responsibility, so it belongs with the surface that owns the behaviour
# rather than in a package minted just to hold it. It also needs no new
# entry in stow.sh's PACKAGES array — one fewer place a fresh-machine
# install can go wrong.
#
# THE PACKAGE STAYS INSTALLED, DELIBERATELY
# `network-manager-applet` remains in install.sh's PACMAN_PKGS (added by
# 15-08, D-15-23): it ships `nm-connection-editor`, which is the wifi
# panel's Advanced target. Only the autostarted *agent* is suppressed, not
# the package. Do not "clean up" the package because this file exists.
#
# EVIDENCE THAT THIS AGENT OWNED THE EXTERNAL DIALOG
# nm-applet (PID 1018 during the UAT session) was the sole registered
# NetworkManager secret agent for the whole boot — the only
# agent-registration line present. On a wrong passphrase the supplicant
# reports a 4-way handshake failure, NetworkManager logs
# `psk mismatch reported by supplicant, asking for new key`, re-enters
# `need-auth`, and issues GetSecrets to that registered agent, which
# answers with its own GTK dialog. NetworkManager does NOT fail an
# activation on a PSK mismatch — it re-asks. That dialog is another
# process's XDG toplevel, and a layer-shell overlay sits unconditionally
# above every XDG toplevel in Hyprland, so no QML change could ever have
# won the z-order fight. Removing the agent is the only fix.
#
# WHY THE KEY BELOW WORKS
# XDG autostart precedence is filename-keyed and
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/autostart shadows /etc/xdg/autostart, so this filename
# must match the system entry byte-for-byte. systemd-xdg-autostart-generator
# honours `Hidden=true` per its own man page and generates no unit at all.
#
# NAMED CONSEQUENCE — READ BEFORE REMOVING THIS FILE
# With this in place there is NO NetworkManager secret agent on this host.
# Any non-quickshell NM client that needs a secret will FAIL rather than
# prompt. That is the intended trade: the wifi panel supplies its own
# passphrases natively, and a silent external prompt is precisely the bug
# G-15-4 closed.
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Name=Network
Exec=nm-applet
Hidden=true
